{
  "abstract": "For prime $n>3$, let $X_n$ be the elliptic surface $y^2=x^3+3(D_n(s)-2)x+(D_n(s)-2)(D_n(s)-6)/2$. An explicit degree-$24n$ curve-square map places its entire transcendental cohomology in a primitive Jacobian factor $A_n$. A binomial cancellation proves this even when geometric genus exceeds one. A sum-of-squares identity and Dirichlet nonvanishing determine the full Hodge group whenever $A_n$ is non-CM, proving HC on all powers and mixed products in distinct degrees. We establish the remaining source condition in degrees seven and eleven through incompatible endomorphism centralizers at good reductions. In degree eleven, automorphism-twisted counts over $13^5$ reconstruct Frobenius over $13^{10}$ while retaining its quadratic coefficient field and nonrational polarization norm. Independent CPU implementations verify the arithmetic. Together with the known degree-five case, this proves HC on every $X_5^a\\times X_7^b\\times X_{11}^c$. In degree five, an explicit fourfold source factor and Schoen's Eisenstein sixfold cycles supply the additional mixed Hodge tensors. We then construct a threefold quotient whose crepant Weierstrass modification exposes a fixed elliptic divisor. Its ramification proves uniruledness and supplies actual curve support for the rank-eight spin half twist. Identifying its Hodge circle with the Kuga–Satake lift and transferring the source cycles proves algebraicity of the canonical Kuga–Satake correspondence for $X_5$. A highest-weight argument also proves generalized Hodge support on all mixed powers of this K3 and its simple spin factor. These results do not use the recent general CM-HC claim. Assuming CM-HC gives a separate surface-product conclusion in every prime degree. Uniform non-CM and the general rational Hodge conjecture remain open.",
